import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.32 War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.32 War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths","xkChars":0,"xkWords":607,"xkLessons":{"D.32":{"title":"D.32 War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths","lead":"Historical explanation retains distinct subjects, mechanisms, and versions. Signature, ratification, legal status, and consequences for life can have different dates.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5121–5138, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-32#section-d-32. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘An accurate documentary answer need not wait for an allocation of overall causal shares.’ Identify the state as a legal subject, government, military, organizations, and populations bearing consequences separately. Realist explanations in terms of resources and security, institutionalist explanations in terms of commitments and monitoring, and constructivist explanations of constitutive status can offer different conditional accounts; they cannot be merged into a single unified state will. [T-main]","Defensive intentions, actual security effects, the other side's judgment, future ability to fulfill commitments, and legitimacy differ. Communication can reduce certain informational uncertainties without automatically resolving future incentives and execution constraints. Declared goodwill does not mean the other side's security truly improves, nor does it alone determine legality. [T-main]","Mobilization must distinguish first participation, repeated participation, sustained organization, concessions, transfer of power, and subsequent life. Support without registration, registration without attendance, withdrawal, and continued participation each have positions in the account. Do not turn earlier outcomes into inevitabilities by looking only at winners' retrospective lists, or count repeated attendances as distinct participants. [T-main]","Analysis of empire and decolonization retains separate channels of trade, conquest, cultural exchange, inequality, and dependence. Formal sovereignty, administrative capacity, economic choice, and citizenship do not establish one another. Leaders' histories cannot replace local actors and internal differences. Real industrial effects also do not substitute for living outcomes among workers, carers, and those unable to migrate. [T-main]","The book uses Khaldun's cooperation and ruling coalitions, Toynbee's conditional responses, Tainter's costs of complexity, and Turchin's feedback hypotheses as navigation in historical dynamics. Their distinct questions are retained without combining them into a universal civilizational cycle or claiming a complete reading of all original works. The maintenance costs of model complexity and the organizational costs of actual societies are not the same quantity. [T-main]","Diminishing marginal returns mean only that additional returns become smaller, not that they are negative, net benefits are negative, or collapse is imminent. Preserve separate versions of the original prediction, later models, authors' retrospective assessments, and independent tests. Performance of a revised model cannot be backfilled into the original prediction, and a retrospective assessment cannot be presented as independent validation. [T-main]","Modernization, industrialization, and global connections do not form a single developmental ladder. Peace distinguishes at least cessation of violence, dispute procedures, political arrangements, responsibility, recovery of everyday life, and reconciliation. Group cooperation does not prove individual psychological recovery. Counterfactuals should alter only evidenced nodes and reestimate downstream effects, rather than present an imagined alternative path as known history. [T-main]","Specific documentary case: the chapter in Volume XIII of the U.S. Department of State's FRUS records that the U.S.–German treaty restoring friendly relations was signed in Berlin on August 25, 1921 and entered into force through exchange of ratifications on November 11. For the United States, the state of war had already been declared ended by the joint resolution of July 2. The German government treated entry into force on November 11 as the turning point, and full diplomatic relations resumed on November 16. [P-FRUS]","A bare question of ‘when it ended’ therefore requires first specifying the legal subject and the event at issue. Signature, entry into force, and restoration of diplomatic relations cannot substitute for one another. Documents can clearly answer these timing questions without first resolving causal shares in every social consequence. Dates cannot establish simultaneous recovery of all residents' lives, trauma, or reconciliation either. [T-main][P-FRUS]","Before comparing historical quantities, check the target and common support, then retain remaining disagreements. Total violent deaths and the violence-related component of excess deaths overlap substantially but are not identical; renaming cannot eliminate substantive differences. Intervals from separate studies cannot be joined into a common confidence interval. Assessing completeness, assumptions for correcting underreporting, and adjustment for missing visits are distinct steps. [T-main]","Signature and ratification, funding commitments, receipt, settlement, and consequences for life likewise require separate evidence. Unsupported endpoints remain unknown; a true upstream stage cannot fill them in. Connections to §§2.4–2.7, §8.1, Parts X–XII, Part XIV, and §16.4 examine causal scope, organizational relationships, aims, and responsibility. [T-main]","The eight entry points are: 32.01, three perspectives in international relations; 32.02, distinct judgments about war, security dilemmas, and deterrence; 32.03, multiple stages of revolution, movements, and mobilization; 32.04, empire, colonialism, decolonization, and state building; 32.05, civilizational rise and fall and different hypotheses of historical dynamics; 32.06, multiple outcomes of modernization, industry, and globalization; 32.07, peace negotiations, reconciliation, and postwar order; and 32.08, contingency, individuals, structure, and bounded counterfactuals. Each retains ordinary people and conditions of execution at the endpoint, rather than replacing specific research with grand propositions. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Specify the subject, event, outcome, and time window at issue.","Separate information about intentions, incentives, capacities, and legitimacy.","Track distinct participants and attendances, power outcomes, and ordinary people's lives.","Separate original predictions, revised models, retrospective assessments, and independent tests.","Before comparing quantities, align targets and common support, then record differences that cannot be removed."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Diminishing marginal returns","leftText":"The additional increment gets smaller.","rightTitle":"Negative marginal returns","rightText":"The increment is below zero; this does not follow automatically."},{"leftTitle":"Signature","leftText":"Completion of the act of signing.","rightTitle":"Ratification and entry into force","rightText":"May have separate dates under the relevant procedures and clauses."},{"leftTitle":"Group cooperation","leftText":"Observed joint activity.","rightTitle":"Individual recovery","rightText":"Psychological and living conditions require individual evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Interval envelope","leftText":"Taking the minimum and maximum across intervals.","rightTitle":"Common confidence interval","rightText":"Requires a common statistical model; an envelope does not automatically have that meaning."}],"boundaries":["Source boundary: diminishing marginal returns do not imply that net benefits are already negative or collapse imminent.","Teaching counterexample: a person attending twice in the same event series cannot count as two distinct participants.","Source boundary: later facts cannot rewrite earlier outcomes as inevitable in advance."],"questions":["Do diminishing returns mean losses have already occurred?","Does identifying when peace occurred require only one date?","Is a retrospective assessment an independent test?","Do six attendances mean six people?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/figures/d32-dates.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Different dates for the 1921 U.S.–German treaty's signature, entry into force, and legal status","href":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv13/ch3"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Different dates for the 1921 U.S.–German treaty's signature, entry into force, and legal status","text":"FRUS records signature on 1921-08-25 and entry into force through exchange of ratifications on 11-11. The United States had already legally declared the state of war ended on 7-2; the German government treated 11-11 as the turning point, and full diplomatic relations resumed on 11-16.","sourceLabel":"U.S. Department of State, FRUS: The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume XIII, Peace between the United States and Germany","sourceUrl":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv13/ch3","sourceLocator":"Printed pages 20–21 of this chapter, web-page lines 58–63; the joint resolution text and approval date appear on pages 18–19 / lines 38–50","boundary":"Supports only the specified documentary events and distinctions among subjects, not simultaneous recovery of group living conditions or individual psychology.","href":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv13/ch3"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.32","start_line":5212,"end_line":5229,"startLine":5212,"endLine":5229}],"outputId":"section-d-32","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: diminishing but still positive","text":"Artificial total benefits are B(n) = 10n−n². For n from 0 to 3, values are 0, 9, 16, and 21; additional benefits 9, 7, and 5 diminish but remain positive. If total costs are C(n) = 2n, net benefits of 0, 7, 12, and 15 also continue increasing.","assumptions":"Discussion is limited to the stated domain n = 0, 1, 2, 3, with fictional benefits and costs in common units.","boundary":"This is not a model of civilization and is not extrapolated to other n values or used to predict collapse."},{"title":"Teaching construction: attendances do not equal people","text":"Artificial participants are A, B, and C at the first event, and B, C, and D at the second. There are six attendances, four distinct people, and two repeat participants. Sustained organization and concessions remain unknown.","assumptions":"Artificial lists and complete records for two events; nonattendees are not presumed opposed.","boundary":"Participation records do not directly prove political success, free consent, or improved living conditions."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: diminishing marginal returns do not imply that net benefits are already negative or collapse imminent.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: a person attending twice in the same event series cannot count as two distinct participants.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: later facts cannot rewrite earlier outcomes as inevitable in advance.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Do diminishing returns mean losses have already occurred?","answer":"No. 9, 7, and 5 remain positive; net values require costs.","basis":"Logic."},{"question":"Does identifying when peace occurred require only one date?","answer":"First specify the event and subject; documentary status differs from consequences for life.","basis":"The historical case."},{"question":"Is a retrospective assessment an independent test?","answer":"No. Mark versions and the status of each test separately.","basis":"Evidential status."},{"question":"Do six attendances mean six people?","answer":"The two artificial lists contain only four distinct participants.","basis":"Deduplication."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/figures/d32-dates.png","alt":"1921: U.S. legal status on July 2, signature on August 25, entry into force on November 11, and diplomatic relations on November 16.","caption":"Specified FRUS documents and compilation; no inference of simultaneous recovery of everyday life."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/figures/d32-returns.png","alt":"Artificial total benefits 0/9/16/21; marginal benefits 9/7/5 remain positive.","caption":"Teaching construction."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/figures/d32-mobilize.png","alt":"Two events, ABC and BCD: six attendances, four distinct people, and two repeat participants.","caption":"Teaching construction."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures show historical documentary dates, marginal returns, and measures of mobilization.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["The complete FRUS volume was not read, nor were all postwar consequences for life studied.","No new comprehensive review of original works in historical dynamics or specific mortality-estimation studies was undertaken."],"taskId":"S-D-32","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.32, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 5033–5050","claim_support":["Historical subjects, mechanisms, versions, marginal benefits, peace documents, quantities, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-FRUS","title":"U.S. Department of State, FRUS: The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume XIII, Peace between the United States and Germany","url":"https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv13/ch3","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1921 events recorded in Volume XIII of the historical documents on the Paris Peace Conference of 1919","locator":"Printed pages 20–21 of this chapter, web-page lines 58–63; the joint resolution text and approval date appear on pages 18–19 / lines 38–50","claim_support":["Signed in Berlin on 1921-08-25; entered into force through exchange of ratifications on 11-11. For the United States, the state of war ended under the joint resolution of 7-2; the German government treated entry into force on 11-11 as the turning point. Full diplomatic relations resumed on 11-16."],"limits":["The lesson author read the specified passages in this chapter and relevant text of the joint resolution, not the whole volume. The official compilation and recorded documents support dates and statuses, not complete psychological or everyday recovery for the population."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.32 War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths; Chinese source lines 5121–5138; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/source/S-D-32.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-32/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"b2c26ef2feed4a0358a1bb30f9ad83c2229c9f822a201925867ea8aa690dd2fe","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-32.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-32.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-32.md"};function l(u,e,p,m,h,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-32" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.32</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-32" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.32 War, Revolution, Empire, Development, and Historical Paths {#section-d-32}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §2.4–2.7, §8.1, §10–12, §14, and §16.4. Research entries cover 32.01–32.08.</p><p>Identify the state as a legal entity, the government, the military, organizations, and the populations that bear the consequences separately. Realism, institutionalism, and constructivism focus on resource security, the monitoring of commitments, and constituted status. These concerns can support different explanations of the conditions at work. Do not combine them into a single will of the state. Defensive intent, actual effects on security, the other side’s assessment, future capability to honor commitments, and normative justification are also distinct. Communication can reduce a type of uncertainty about information. It does not automatically resolve future incentives or enforcement.</p><p>In mobilization, distinguish initial participation, repeated participation, sustained organizing, obtaining concessions, transfer of power, and subsequent living conditions. Supporting without signing up, signing up without taking part, dropping out, and continuing to participate are distinct positions. A later victory does not establish that the outcome was inevitable at the time. Analyses of empire and decolonization retain separate channels for trade, conquest, cultural exchange, and unequal dependence. Formal sovereignty, administrative capability, economic choices, and citizenship rights cannot serve as evidence for one another. Histories centered on leaders cannot stand in for local actors or internal differences.</p><p>For historical dynamics, retain Khaldun’s account of cooperation and the reproduction of ruling coalitions, Toynbee’s account of responses to conditions, Tainter’s question about the costs of complexity, and Turchin’s feedback hypothesis. These do not add up to a unified civilizational cycle. Diminishing marginal returns do not imply negative marginal returns, much less negative net returns or imminent collapse. The cost of maintaining a complex model must also be kept distinct from the costs of actual social organization. Retain separate versions for the original prediction, later models, retrospective assessments by the authors, and independent tests.</p><p>Modernization, industrialization, and global connections are distinct from one another and do not make up a single development ladder. Actual effects on industry cannot stand in for outcomes for workers, caregivers, or people who cannot move. Peace encompasses at least the cessation of violence, procedures for handling disputes, political arrangements, responsibility, recovery of living conditions, and reconciliation. Cooperation between groups does not establish individual psychological recovery. In a counterfactual, limit changes to nodes for which there is evidence and reassess downstream effects. Retain the conditional roles of historical figures, ordinary people, and implementation at the end of the chain. Do not write the counterfactual as another history whose course is already known.</p><p>When comparing historical quantities, first check the target and the range of common support. Preserve disagreements that remain. The total number of violent deaths and the violence-related component of excess deaths overlap substantially and are not identical. Renaming them cannot remove the gap, and the intervals from different studies cannot be stitched together into a common confidence interval. Diagnostic completeness, assumptions used to correct for underreporting in practice, and adjustments for missed interviews belong to different steps. Signing, approval, funding commitments, receipt, settlement, and consequences for people’s lives each require their own evidence. An accurate answer about the documentary record can stand before the overall causal share has been established.</p><p>Research topics. 32.01 Realism, liberal institutionalism, and constructivism. 32.02 The origins of war, the security dilemma, and deterrence. 32.03 Revolution, social movements, and collective mobilization. 32.04 Empire, colonialism, decolonization, and state-building. 32.05 The rise and fall of civilizations and historical dynamics (Khaldun, Toynbee, Tainter, Turchin, and others). 32.06 Modernization, industrialization, and globalization. 32.07 Peace, negotiation, reconciliation, and the postwar order. 32.08 Contingency, historical figures, structure, and counterfactual history.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.32"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
